For an L6-level Java/Distributed Systems interview, 

I would learn Virtual Threads at three levels simultaneously:

          Java developer level — how to write and debug code with them.
          Architect level — when they improve throughput/latency and when they do not.
          JVM level — how a virtual thread maps to carriers, scheduling, blocking, parking, and OS threads.

For a Java 21 interview, the important concept is Spring Boot 3.x's virtual-thread support, 
while the exact Spring Boot minor version should be checked against the project's dependency stack. 

The Java-side virtual-thread implementation is a Java 21 feature.
================================================================================================================================================

1. The L6 mental model:

        The most important sentence to remember is:
        
                A virtual thread is a JVM-managed, lightweight thread whose execution is multiplexed over 
                a much smaller pool of OS-backed carrier threads.


Traditional Java:
                      Application Task
                            │
                            ▼
                      Java Platform Thread
                            │
                            ▼
                      OS Thread
                            │
                            ▼
                           CPU

Virtual threads:
                       JVM
                        │
              ┌─────────┴─────────┐
              │                   │
       Virtual Thread         Virtual Thread
              │                   │
              └─────────┬─────────┘
                        ▼
                  Carrier Thread
                  (OS-backed)
                        │
                        ▼
                       CPU

And potentially:

                100,000 virtual threads
                          │
                          │ multiplexed onto
                          ▼
                 ~N carrier threads
                          │
                          ▼
                     OS threads
                          │
                          ▼
                        CPUs

The critical architectural insight is:
          Virtual threads solve the cost of having large numbers of concurrent tasks waiting on I/O. They do not create more CPU.
================================================================================================================================================
Defination of what is virtual thread: interview answer:

              “Virtual threads are a very powerful concurrency model that became a permanent part of Java in Java 21. 
              The key idea is that they allow a JVM application to handle a very large number of concurrent tasks without 
              requiring a one-to-one relationship between application threads and OS threads.
              
              This significantly changes the scalability boundary of the traditional thread-per-request model, 
              particularly for highly concurrent, I/O-bound workloads.
              
              But there is an important architectural caveat: virtual threads don't magically increase system capacity. 
              CPU, database connections, network bandwidth, downstream services, connection pools, 
              and other scarce resources still remain the actual bottlenecks.
              
              So, when introducing virtual threads, I would treat it as a change in the application's 
              concurrency model—not simply as a way to create more threads. I would pair it with 
              explicit concurrency controls, backpressure, strong observability, and end-to-end capacity testing.
              
              In short, virtual threads let us scale concurrency much more efficiently, 
              but they don't remove resource constraints. They move the bottleneck; they don't eliminate it.”


The L6-level statement to remember:
                "Virtual threads make concurrency cheap; they don't make resources infinite."

And therefore:
              "When adopting virtual threads, I would separate task concurrency from resource concurrency.
              I can have hundreds of thousands of virtual threads representing work, 
              while explicitly controlling access to scarce resources such as CPU, database connections, 
              connection pools, and downstream services. 

              I would use backpressure to prevent overload, 
              observability to identify the real bottleneck, and end-to-end capacity testing to validate 
              the sustainable throughput of the complete system."

================================================================================================================================================
40. L6 architecture question

          Imagine interviewer asks:          
                    "We have 50,000 concurrent requests and our Spring Boot application uses a fixed thread pool of 500. 
                    Would you replace it with virtual threads?"
          
          A strong answer:          
                    "I would first characterize the workload. If the requests are 
                    predominantly I/O-bound and spend significant time waiting on JDBC, HTTP or 
                    other blocking operations, virtual threads are a strong candidate because they 
                    allow us to preserve imperative code while increasing the number of concurrently 
                    waiting tasks without requiring one expensive platform thread per request. 
                    However, I would not treat virtual threads as the capacity solution by themselves. 
                    I would benchmark end-to-end throughput and latency and inspect downstream constraints 
                    such as database connection pools, HTTP connection pools, rate limits and synchronization hotspots. 
                    I would also look for virtual-thread pinning and excessive ThreadLocal state."
          
          That's an L6-style answer.


